  This logger's first call site is a login function that takes a password as an argument, and the current code already
  logs the username in plaintext on every attempt. Once logs are POSTed to a server, anything the logger touches becomes
  data you've collected, stored, and have to protect. A redaction rule added after the fact doesn't retroactively clean
  the logs you already shipped.

  - [1mDeny-by-default allowlist on the remote transport[0m — only fields explicitly declared safe get serialized and sent;
    everything else is dropped, whatever it's called. Buys the property that a future careless [38;5;153mlog.info('ctx', formData)
[39m    cannot leak, because the default is to drop. Costs a little friction at each call site: you name what you want.
    This is the choice that's genuinely hard to reverse — retrofitting deny-by-default onto call sites written against a
    permissive logger means auditing every one of them.
  - [1mDenylist / key scrubbing[0m — log whatever's passed, recursively strip keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m,
    [38;5;153mauthorization[39m. Buys convenience; ergonomics stay identical to [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m. Costs: it fails open. A key named [38;5;153mpwd[39m,
    [38;5;153mcredentials[39m, or a password nested in an array of form entries sails straight through, and you find out from the
    logs.
  - [1mNo automatic redaction[0m — call-site discipline only. Cheapest to build, and the failure mode is plaintext passwords
    in your log store.

  [1mMy recommendation: deny-by-default allowlist for the remote transport, with the console transport left verbose.[0m The
  split matters — on a user's own machine the console showing their own username costs nothing, so local debugging stays
  rich, while the allowlist applies at exactly the boundary where data leaves the device. I'd also log a stable
  anonymous session ID rather than the username, so you can still correlate a user's sequence of failures without your
  log store becoming a list of who tried to log in and when.
